  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2026 editorial introduction to the Journal of Research on Research (J\u00b7ROR) is already visible in the scholarly record through open-access availability and a small amount of early web discussion, but it has not yet accrued citations. The current evidence points to initial community notice rather than established downstream scholarly uptake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article \u201cIntroducing the Journal of Research on Research (J\u00b7ROR): aligning constellations and communities\u201d serves as a launch piece for a new venue in research-on-research and metascience. It is published in the Journal of Research on Research in 2026 and is authored by G. M. Derrick, Serge P. J. M. Horbach, Bart Penders, and T. Ross-Hellauer, bringing together contributors associated with higher education, sociology, health, and research infrastructure perspectives. The record suggests an editorial or perspective-style contribution rather than a conventional empirical research paper.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Its strongest measurable signal is accessibility: the work is available open access, with repository instances recorded in the Netherlands as well as publisher-hosted access. That kind of distribution supports discoverability and reuse, especially for a journal introduction that is meant to orient a community and define a publication space. The metadata also indicates dataset-related processing in Crossref, suggesting structured citation and indexing support, although that is not the same as evidence of reuse by other researchers.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">On the attention side, the article has attracted a small amount of web discussion: one Substack mention and one blog mention are recorded, including commentary in metascience-oriented spaces and a Retraction Watch roundup. These are early signs that the piece entered conversations around research reform and journal infrastructure, but they remain limited in scope. No citation accumulation is yet visible in OpenAlex, and scite reports no citing statements at this time.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the record fits an emerging, community-facing publication: open, indexed, and noticed by a few specialized outlets, but not yet supported by a deeper citation trail. Because the evidence base is still thin, the most defensible reading is that the article has begun to circulate within its intended audience, while its longer-term scholarly influence has yet to emerge.</p>",
